Add a logging middleware in app.js before or after sessionUser, depending on what you want to capture.

**Before sessionUser** — logs all requests including unauthenticated ones. Useful if you want to see rejection attempts:

```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path}`);
  next();
});
app.use(sessionUser);
```

**After sessionUser** — logs only authenticated requests, with user context available:

```javascript
app.use(sessionUser);
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path} user=${req.user.id}`);
  next();
});
app.use("/accounts", accounts);
app.use("/orders", orders);
```

To capture response status and timing, wrap the response:

```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  res.on("finish", () => {
    const duration = Date.now() - start;
    console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path} ${res.statusCode} ${duration}ms user=${req.user.id}`);
  });
  next();
});
```

For production, replace console.log with a proper logging library (Winston, Pino, Bunyan). Be careful not to log request bodies if they contain passwords or billing data.

